```java
class TextEditorModel {
    private StringBuilder text; // The text content
    private StringBuilder undoBuffer; // Buffer to store undo units
    private boolean isRecording; // Flag to indicate if recording is in progress
    private boolean mIgnoreXmlUpdate; // Flag to ignore XML updates

    public TextEditorModel() {
        text = new StringBuilder();
        undoBuffer = new StringBuilder();
        isRecording = false;
        mIgnoreXmlUpdate = false;
    }

    void beginRecording() {
        isRecording = true;
    }

    void endRecording(TextEditorModel model) {
        if (isRecording) {
            undoBuffer.append(text.toString()).append("\n"); // Save the current state as an undo unit
            isRecording = false; // Reset the recording state
        }
        model.undoReverseCount = 0; // Reset the undo reverse count
        model.mIgnoreXmlUpdate = mIgnoreXmlUpdate; // Set the ignore XML update flag
    }

    void undo() {
        if (undoBuffer.length() > 0) {
            int lastNewlineIndex = undoBuffer.lastIndexOf("\n");
            if (lastNewlineIndex != -1) {
                text = new StringBuilder(undoBuffer.substring(0, lastNewlineIndex)); // Revert the text
                undoBuffer.delete(lastNewlineIndex + 1, undoBuffer.length()); // Remove the last undo unit
            }
        }
    }
}
```

In the solution, the `TextEditorModel` class maintains the text content, an undo buffer to store undo units, and flags for recording and ignoring XML updates. The `beginRecording()` method sets the recording flag, and the `endRecording()` method saves the recorded changes as an undo unit and resets the recording state. The `undo()` method reverts the text to the state before the last recorded changes by retrieving and removing the last undo unit from the undo buffer.

