Your implementation should adhere to the following requirements:
1. If the input ArrayList is empty, the method should return an empty ArrayList.
2. If any of the polygons in the input ArrayList have an invalid number of sides (less than 3), the method should throw an Exception.
3. The method should sort the polygons in ascending order based on their total perimeter, where the total perimeter of a polygon is the sum of the lengths of all its sides.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.Comparator;

public class PolygonSorter {
    public ArrayList<Polygon> sortByPerimeter(ArrayList<Polygon> polygonArrayList) throws Exception {
        if (polygonArrayList.isEmpty()) {
            return new ArrayList<>();
        }

        for (Polygon polygon : polygonArrayList) {
            if (polygon.getSideLengths().size() < 3) {
                throw new Exception("Invalid polygon: less than 3 sides");
            }
        }

        Collections.sort(polygonArrayList, Comparator.comparingDouble(this::calculateTotalPerimeter));

        return polygonArrayList;
    }

    private double calculateTotalPerimeter(Polygon polygon) {
        return polygon.getSideLengths().stream().mapToDouble(Double::doubleValue).sum();
    }
}
```

In the solution, we first handle the edge case of an empty input ArrayList by returning an empty ArrayList. Then, we iterate through the input ArrayList to check if any polygon has less than 3 sides, throwing an Exception if found. Finally, we use the `Collections.sort` method along with a custom comparator to sort the polygons based on their total perimeter, calculated using the `calculateTotalPerimeter` method.

