```java
import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.JsonNode;
import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper;

public class JsonViewParser {
    public String parse(String json, String view) {
        try {
            ObjectMapper mapper = new ObjectMapper();
            JsonNode rootNode = mapper.readTree(json);
            String[] keys = view.split("\\.");
            JsonNode currentNode = rootNode;

            for (String key : keys) {
                if (currentNode.isArray()) {
                    int index = Integer.parseInt(key);
                    currentNode = currentNode.get(index);
                } else {
                    currentNode = currentNode.get(key);
                }
            }

            return currentNode.asText();
        } catch (Exception e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
            return null;
        }
    }
}
```

In the solution, we use the Jackson library to parse the JSON string into a `JsonNode` tree structure. We then traverse the tree based on the keys in the view string to reach the desired value. If a key represents an array index, we parse it as an integer and access the corresponding element in the array. Finally, we return the text value of the current node as the result.

