import java.util.concurrent.locks.Lock;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

public class LockManager {
    private final Lock lock = new ReentrantLock();

    /**
     * Acquires the lock on the specified target resource for the given user.
     *
     * @param target   The resource on which the lock needs to be acquired.
     * @param username The name of the user who wants to acquire the lock.
     * @return true if the lock was acquired; otherwise false.
     */
    public boolean acquireLock(Lockable target, String username) {
        try {
            lock.lock(); // Ensures thread-safe acquisition of the lock
            String currentUser = AuthUtil.getRemoteUser(); // Retrieve the username of the current user
            if (currentUser != null && currentUser.equals(username)) {
                return target.acquireLock(username); // Delegate lock acquisition to the Lockable resource
            } else {
                return false; // User authentication failed, unable to acquire the lock
            }
        } finally {
            lock.unlock(); // Release the lock in a finally block to ensure it is always released
        }
    }
}
```

In the solution, the `LockManager` class uses a `ReentrantLock` to ensure thread-safe acquisition of the lock. The `acquireLock` method first acquires the lock using the `lock` object, then retrieves the username of the current user using `AuthUtil#getRemoteUser()`. If the current user matches the provided `username`, the method delegates the lock acquisition to the `Lockable` resource by calling its `acquireLock` method. If the user authentication fails or the lock cannot be acquired, the method returns `false`. Finally, the lock is released in a `finally` block to ensure it is always released, even in case of exceptions.

