import java.lang.Math;

interface IGeoData {
    double calculateDistance(Location loc);
    boolean isSameLocation(Location loc);
}

class Location {
    double latitude;
    double longitude;
    double altitude;

    public Location(double latitude, double longitude, double altitude) {
        this.latitude = latitude;
        this.longitude = longitude;
        this.altitude = altitude;
    }
}

class GeoData extends Location implements IGeoData {
    public GeoData(double latitude, double longitude, double altitude) {
        super(latitude, longitude, altitude);
    }

    @Override
    public double calculateDistance(Location loc) {
        double earthRadius = 6371; // in kilometers
        double lat1 = Math.toRadians(this.latitude);
        double lon1 = Math.toRadians(this.longitude);
        double lat2 = Math.toRadians(loc.latitude);
        double lon2 = Math.toRadians(loc.longitude);

        double dLat = lat2 - lat1;
        double dLon = lon2 - lon1;

        double a = Math.pow(Math.sin(dLat / 2), 2) + Math.cos(lat1) * Math.cos(lat2) * Math.pow(Math.sin(dLon / 2), 2);
        double c = 2 * Math.atan2(Math.sqrt(a), Math.sqrt(1 - a));

        return earthRadius * c;
    }

    @Override
    public boolean isSameLocation(Location loc) {
        return this.latitude == loc.latitude && this.longitude == loc.longitude && this.altitude == loc.altitude;
    }
}
```

In this solution, the `IGeoData` interface defines the methods `calculateDistance` and `isSameLocation` for handling geographical data. The `Location` class provides the basic attributes for representing a geographical location. The `GeoData` class extends `Location` and implements `IGeoData`, completing the implementation by adding the necessary methods to calculate distance and compare locations. The `calculateDistance` method uses the Haversine formula to calculate the distance between two geographical locations. The `isSameLocation` method checks if two locations are the same based on their latitude, longitude, and altitude.

